# Line endings are part of this project's contract, not a formatting preference.
#
# Every file git detects as text is stored LF in the repository *and* checked
# out LF in the working tree, on every platform. Without this, a Windows
# checkout under `core.autocrlf=true` — the default this machine had — hands
# CRLF to code that reads bytes. Rust's `std::fs::read_to_string` performs no
# translation, so a parser splitting on a marker followed by "\n" finds nothing
# in a file that visibly contains the marker.
#
# That is not hypothetical. `tools/straf3-lab/tests/pinned.rs` failed exactly
# this way on the first Windows run: 2735 of 2735 line endings in
# `docs/movement-lab.md` were CRLF in the working tree and LF in the index, so
# the split on "```tsv\n" missed a section sitting at line 523. The panic it
# raises advises regenerating the document — which would have overwritten the
# published measurement baseline the movement verdict rests on, to "fix" a
# line-ending artefact that was never in the committed bytes.
#
# For a project whose entire premise is that the same inputs produce the same
# bytes, the working tree disagreeing with the index on the primary development
# machine is a defect rather than an inconvenience.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never converted under any setting. Git's heuristic already classifies
# most of these correctly; stating it means correctness does not depend on the
# heuristic getting a new file type right.
#
# `*.s3d` is the load-bearing one and is listed before any such file exists.
# A run artefact is a byte-exact record read against a magic, a format version
# and fixed-width commands (`crates/straf3-replay/src/codec.rs`). End-of-line
# conversion inside one would corrupt the run while leaving a plausible file
# behind, and the resulting ghost-load refusal would present as a determinism
# bug rather than as what it was.
*.s3d   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.png   binary
*.ppm   binary
